      "text": "{\"alpha\": \"9d8172402d\", \"beta\": \"b3066855d6\", \"gamma\": \"2cbc05a779\"}\n\n1. Soil Assessment and Preparation\nThe foundation of any successful community garden is healthy soil. Before planting a single seed, it is crucial to conduct a comprehensive soil assessment. This process begins with obtaining a soil test from a local agricultural extension service or a certified laboratory. The test will reveal the pH level, nutrient content, and potential contaminants. Most vegetables thrive in slightly acidic to neutral soil, typically between a pH of 6.0 and 7.0. If the soil is too acidic, lime can be added to raise the pH; if it is too alkaline, sulfur or peat moss can lower it. Beyond pH, the texture of the soil matters significantly. Sandy soils drain quickly but hold few nutrients, while clay soils retain water and nutrients but can become waterlogged and compacted. To improve sandy soil, incorporate organic matter such as compost, aged manure, or leaf mold. This increases the soil’s ability to retain moisture and nutrients. For clay soils, adding organic matter helps break up the dense structure, improving aeration and drainage. In a community setting, it is often beneficial to build raised beds if the native soil is poor or contaminated. Raised beds allow for complete control over the growing medium, which can be a mix of topsoil, compost, and perlite. Additionally, consider the history of the site. If the land was previously used for industrial purposes or treated with heavy pesticides, a thorough environmental assessment is necessary to ensure safety. Once the soil is prepared, it should be left to settle for a few weeks before planting. This allows the organic matter to decompose further and the microbial life to establish itself. Regularly adding compost to the soil each season will maintain its fertility and structure, ensuring that the garden remains productive for years to come.\n\n2. Crop Selection and Layout\nChoosing the right crops